 "observations": "In this run we observed one-module-per-stage files (ck1 step 3) that each later session read almost wholesale before editing (ck2 steps 2,4,5; ck3 steps 2,4,5; ck4 steps 2-7; ck5 steps 2-10), so every edited place was first shown by a reading sweep (L3). Each session added new modules for its feature (schema/params/casts at ck2 steps 12-19; inputs/badrecords/loader at ck3 steps 8-11; ingest/joins at ck4 steps 14,20; versions/udfs/state/metadata at ck5 steps 14-21) and changed existing code mainly at call points and signatures. Extraction happened at ck2 step 20 and ck4 steps 17-18 and 28. In ck4 step 15 ingest.py wrapped the private inputs._scan, and its file expansion repeats inputs.resolve logic. Verification was always hand runs on throwaway fixtures in /tmp (ck1 steps 11-13; ck2 steps 28-30; ck3 steps 16-21; ck4 steps 22-30; ck5 steps 23-26). It never wrote a test suite. Only ck2 saved a copy of the previous code to diff old-mode behaviour against (ck2 steps 7,30). ck1 wrote DESIGN.md (step 2). All seven failures were coded F2: in each, the test asserts behaviour the spec leaves unstated or states differently. At ck3 step 21 the agent chose content sniffing for a file with no extension, where the test expects exit 3. It dropped an invalid timestamp at ck3 and dropped full-join rows with a null projected timestamp at ck4 and ck5 per the spec. It did not recurse into a directory source when recursive was unset (ck4, ck5), and it returned null for an empty sum (ck5)."
